A transport, a *netlayer*, is how a session's bytes actually move between two
peers. pancake treats it as a contract, not a built-in: the core hands each frame
to whichever netlayer a [locator]($link.page('concepts'))'s transport symbol names,
and never learns which one carried it. Three residents ship today.

## A transport is a resident, not a privilege

The driver holds a registry of netlayers and dispatches `connectVia` and
`acceptVia` on the transport symbol it reads off a locator, over one
transport-generic `Connection`. Every resident funnels through that same
`Connection`, a reader, a writer, and a close handle, so it reuses the one
framing core instead of reinventing it. The proof the contract holds is that the
in-process and iroh residents each registered as new files, with no edit to the
driver or the core: a new transport is a new resident, not a new privilege.

| Transport | Symbol | Channel | Addressed by | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP | `tcp` | cleartext TCP, no TLS | `host:port` | a real network, or interop with Goblins over TCP |
| In-process | `inproc` | in-memory byte pipes, no sockets | a name within one process | tests and demos that run both peers in one process |
| iroh | `iroh` | encrypted QUIC (separate artifact) | a self-authenticating node id in an `ocapn://` URI | you want an encrypted channel today, or peer-to-peer reach |

### TCP

The default real-network transport, and the one that addresses a Goblins peer. It
is cleartext, no TLS, because `tcp` has to speak the same wire as Goblins' cleartext
`tcp`; confidentiality is a separate netlayer's job, not a TLS upgrade waiting to land
on this one. It is proven against a
live [Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) vat over a real socket, then
re-exposed over the C ABI as blocking
endpoint handles. Two I/O paths sit above it: a simple blocking pump for
cooperative request/response, and the completion loop (io_uring on Linux, kqueue on
macOS and BSD) for many connections without a thread apiece.

### In-process (inproc)

No sockets at all. A switchboard wires a dialer and a listener through a pair of
in-memory byte pipes, one per direction, so a full OCapN conversation runs inside a
single process. It exists to prove the netlayer contract is not accidentally
socket-shaped, and it is what the examples and tests use to drive both halves of a
conversation without binding a port.

### iroh

An encrypted transport: [QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html), carried
by an [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) Rust sidecar staticlib and kept in a
*separate optional library*, `libpancake_iroh`, so the base `libpancake` stays pure
Zig. A peer is reached by its self-authenticating Ed25519 endpoint id in an
`ocapn://<nodeid>.iroh` URI rather than a `host:port`, and `zig build iroh-interop`
proves two pancake peers holding a full keyed conversation over real iroh endpoints:
handshake, fetch, and deliver/resolve. It serves the blocking path today; peer
discovery and relays are the open frontier, so a dial currently needs explicit
direct-address hints.

## Encryption is the netlayer's job

Confidentiality is something a netlayer provides, not a flag the API sets. The
contract a transport meets is only "produce a connection over a stream," and it says
nothing about whether that stream is encrypted, so each netlayer answers that for
itself. Plain `tcp` is cleartext because it has to match the `tcp` netlayer Goblins
speaks, byte for byte. `iroh` is encrypted because its QUIC channel is keyed. A
confidential TCP, TLS over a socket, would be another netlayer under the same
contract, with its own transport symbol. Nothing forbids it; it just is not written
yet. So "encrypted" is not a synonym for iroh: iroh is the one encrypted netlayer that
ships, not the definition of encryption.

iroh does carry one wrinkle of its own. It lives in a separate compiled artifact,
`libpancake_iroh`, that you link in addition to the base, and that is not encryption
modeled as a build step. It is iroh being a Rust library, kept out of `libpancake` so
the base stays pure Zig and dependency-free; a pure-Zig TLS netlayer would need no
second artifact at all. In the bindings iroh shows up as `connect_iroh` /
`listen_iroh` beside the TCP verbs, and the only difference a consumer sees is
addressing: a node id and hints in an `ocapn://` URI instead of a host and port.

Where this is headed, an encrypted, NAT-traversing netlayer behind the same contract
as TCP, lives in the
[vision](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/vision.md).
